The model inventory contains 186 classifiers, 419 direct features and 70 declared operations. Construction covers 171 concrete and eight abstract classes; seven enums are separate. The language inventory contains 735 unique grammar IDs, 848 AST tuples and 82 export-dispatch entries, plus required helper/inherited closure. Split grammar ownership requires every assigned owner to qualify. The 44 view-provider batches assign 175 sources. Assignment, implementation, native qualification and acceptance must remain distinguishable for each obligation.

## Retain the specific repair and qualification gaps

- **RR09, then RR10:** qualify graph-index and owned-peer work on 256/1024/4096-node wide/deep graphs, then resource-scoped iterative naming on an 8,192-reference chain and cycles. Capture work/copy counters, cancellation and RSS through actual query/name callers. The held-source handoff remains required.
- **RR03/C01g guard coverage:** isolate 128/129 distinct-resource and manifest-row cases, 32-MiB aggregate limits, independently reachable node/index/walk limits, exact pointer/string boundaries, filesystem refusal and interior cancellation. A count test confounded by duplicate paths does not isolate the count guard. Explain dominated valid-input limits separately from executed boundary evidence; malformed lexical streams still need their own disposition. Retain output-transport failure semantics. These are qualifications within existing obligations, not a claim that all guards have been exercised.
- **RR16b:** bind the complete candidate, command, manifest/feature/target, generator, fixture, tool and external-input closure. Parse outcomes and obligation/case coverage; validate real Git and built-artifact identities, every sealed artifact's committed bytes, teardown and generated freshness. Hash validity or accepted labels alone cannot establish behavior.
- **RR17 after RR16:** wire the full current-source Rust CI matrix and RR16b enforcement into the actual entry point. Negative controls must reject a missing manifest/tool/test/input, stale generation and failed or unrun evidence. CI results inform independent feature acceptance.
- **UI qualification:** close missing glyphs, dense browsing and identifier disclosure; exercise empty/loading/populated/error/read-only/pending states, local scrolling and focus after repaint. Add other browsers, declared devices and actual spoken assistive-technology tasks. Native mobile overflow and clipped labels are defects to improve, not parity behavior to reproduce.

## Verify each PR and keep the evidence useful

Use one isolated worktree and `codex/` branch per PR, with at most three source authors and one coordinator. Keep each ordinary PR within 400 changed handwritten production lines and 900 authored lines including tests; count additions and deletions, helpers, imports and coordinator wiring. Development-tool paths do not create a blanket exemption. Preserve an already approved exception explicitly. Split broader work into reviewed child IDs before coding; never invent a child only after exceeding the budget. Shared manifests/locks, crate exports, routes, registries and application assembly belong to the coordinator. Existing raw/factory/initializer and validation holds remain in force until explicit handoff.

Each PR normally has an implementation/regression commit and a separate evidence/disposition commit. Add an earlier contract/fixture commit where the source boundary is new. For a repair, reproduce the failure where feasible; retain the old failure when the fix passes. Before merging, check the exact reviewed diff, current prerequisites, review threads, hosted check status and expected head. Verify the resulting main tree afterward. Report absent hosted checks as absent, not as a CI pass.

Run focused formatting, locked/offline tests, all-target Clippy and applicable native/WASM checks with one compiler job and one test thread. Bind source before/after, tools, fixtures and external inputs to the receipt. Exercise real downstream calls. Browser evidence requires real events, meaningful rendered content, screenshots, focus/accessibility state, console/page errors and resulting model/history/representation artifacts. Tests and screenshots support their named cases; they do not alone establish usability.

For changed performance paths, use release builds and restore identical model, selection, drafts and history between repetitions. Record two warmups and seven measured samples per named action/viewport, with all raw values and min/median/max. Use at least 30 measured samples and a declared estimator for p95. Keep event-to-visible latency, main-thread tasks, server/database time, allocation requests, peak live heap and RSS separate. Compare equivalent native/Rust workloads before making speed claims.

| Workload | Provisional event-to-visible target |
| --- | --- |
| Continuous feedback | 16–50 ms |
| Ordinary authoring | 50–150 ms |
| Named dense authoring | 150–350 ms |
| Named large import/export/recovery | 250–1,000 ms |

These [policy ranges](performance-policy.json) guide review. Faster results are acceptable; excursions need context and scoped follow-up rather than an automatic timing veto. Integrity quotas, data preservation, bounded admission and usable controls remain required. A result inside a band does not establish usability or native equivalence.

Resource ramps must investigate native-documented capacity: 10-MB models, 150,000 semantic elements, 2,000 representations/project, 1,000 elements/representation, 1,000 projects and 80 concurrent users. Record the definitions, configuration, failure/recovery behavior and tested limits; these values are qualification hypotheses rather than an already supported Rust capacity claim.

## Completion requires these distinct decisions

A scope is **ready to implement** only when its required implementation inputs, review budget, interface and nonoverlapping ownership are admitted. A scope is **locally verified** when the exact source passes its named checks; this does not establish native equivalence, integrated UI behavior or durability.

A PR is **landed** when the reviewed change and actual prerequisites are on main, affected checks/callers apply to the resulting composition, and the remote landing tree is verified. A feature is **accepted** only when every required source obligation has an independently reviewed runtime disposition, including applicable native, consuming-workflow, failure, UI and resource evidence. An intentional UX improvement needs its behavior record and compatibility impact; it cannot erase a mismatch by permissive normalization.

The program is complete when C01's required scope is closed; every primary and discovered obligation is accepted on one target/candidate closure; connected workflows, interoperability, recovery, usability and capacity gates pass; and clean release rebuild, packages/processes, notices and upgrade/rollback evidence are accepted. Required unsupported, unresolved, unimplemented or unrun work prevents completion. Unchanged-component evidence may be reused only with explicit unchanged-input proof; affected/composed workflows must cover the final candidate.

For each completion decision, retain the source/artifact revisions, requirement and case IDs, commands, raw results, differences, failed attempts, independent review, remaining limits and resulting GitHub main tree. Name any required unrun platform or workflow explicitly. A plan lock proves document integrity; it does not close a feature.

The current completion checker validates planning structure and evidence bindings; stronger behavioral enforcement remains RR16b/RR17 work. An expected `OPEN` result is an honest status check, not a completed program. Review or amend this snapshot deliberately when priorities or progress change; requirement changes continue through the versioned completion-contract process.

To check this freeze from the repository root, run `python3 porting/rust-parity-plan-20261026/remaining-work/verify_snapshot.py --self-test`. To check the adopted plan and report open acceptance gates, run `python3 porting/rust-parity-plan-20261026/tools/verify_plan.py --self-test --check-completion`; exit 2 currently means parity remains open. These commands assess documentation and planning integrity, not feature behavior.
